<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teresa, Countess of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1080–<a href="/years/1130">1130</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Teresa of León · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teresa was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> who, with her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a>, held the County of Portugal from 1096. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s death in 1112 she ruled alone, styling herself queen, and allied with the Galician Trava family. Her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> defeated her supporters at São Mamede in 1128 and drove her into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, where she died in 1130.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teresa-countess-of-portugal.jpg" alt="Teresa, Countess of Portugal" width="320" height="671" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theresa_of_Portugal_(1080-1130)_mini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Teresa was born around 1080, the illegitimate daughter of King Alfonso VI of León by Jimena Muñoz. In 1093 her father married her to a French nobleman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a>, a nephew of Queen Constance who was giving the king military help against the Muslims on the Portuguese march. Alfonso VI entrusted the county of Portugal to Teresa and Henry in 1096. After the king's death in 1109 they went on governing the lands south of the Minho as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mondego">Mondego</a>, and by December 1111 they also governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry died in 1112, leaving Teresa to deal with the political and military situation. She took on the government herself, first occupying herself mainly with her southern lands, recently reconquered from the Muslims as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mondego">Mondego</a>. Pope Paschal II, recognising her defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>, called her queen in a bull of 18 June 1116, and she is explicitly called queen in a document of 1117, which leads some to call her the first monarch of Portugal. She fought her half-sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urraca-of-leon-and-castile">Queen Urraca</a> in 1116 and 1120, and allied herself with Fernando Pérez, Count of Traba, the most powerful Galician nobleman, who had rejected his first wife to marry her openly.</p><p>In 1121 Teresa was besieged and captured at Lanhoso. The Treaty of Lanhoso, arranged with help from the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a>, freed her on condition that she hold the County of Portugal as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Kingdom of León, though she kept her royal title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1128 the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> and the main Portuguese nobles were tired of her Galician alliance. They rebelled, and her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> defeated her troops at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sao-mamede">Battle of São Mamede</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guimaraes">Guimarães</a>. He sent her, with the Count of Traba and their children, into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, near the Portuguese border, where she died on 11 November 1130. Afonso Henriques succeeded her and eventually led Portugal to full independence as a kingdom. With Fernando Pérez she had two daughters, Teresa and Sancha, and with Henry four children, among them Afonso Henriques, who was born in 1109.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theresa, Countess of Portugal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Portugal grew out of the County of Portugal, the land between the Minho and the Mondego that Alfonso VI of León granted in the 1090s to Henry of Burgundy, husband of his daughter <b>Teresa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1096 Alfonso VI of León recreated it, now including Coimbra, for Henry of Burgundy, husband of his daughter <b>Teresa</b>, who ruled after Henry's death in 1112.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-portugal">County of Portugal</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Póvoa de Lanhoso, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Galicia, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.57,-8.27&amp;year=1112" aria-label="Póvoa de Lanhoso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4349 682 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Teresa, Countess of Portugal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4799 952h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4799 952h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.57,-8.27&amp;year=1112">What was here in 1112? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
